8516	8521	8529	8530	THE CONFIDENCE	633	" The PUBLIC EXECUTIONER , "
8552	8562	8564	8565	the policeman	1274	" I see that you are surprised , sir , "
8567	8590	8564	8565	the policeman	1274	" but could n't you guess where you was when you saw the room filled with gibbets , real or in pictures ? "
8591	8606	8608	8608	Richard	612	" It never struck me who the owner of those terrific symbols might be , "
8610	8641	8608	8608	Richard	612	" I concluded that some man of morbid taste dwelt there ; but not for one moment did I imagine that I was in the presence of the public executioner . "
8642	8658	8660	8661	the policeman	1274	" Did you ever see such a horrible - looking object as his son is ? "
8663	8693	8608	8608	Richard	612	" Poor creature — he is greatly to be pitied ! Surely his father can not in reality have conferred upon him the name by which you called him ? "
8694	8847	8608	8608	Richard	612	" I do n't suppose that Gibbet is his real name , sir , but it is the only one I ever heard him called by . You see , sir , Smithers wishes to bring the lad up to the same line : he wants an assistant , and he thinks that Gibbet is old enough to help him . Besides , there 's plenty of work always after Assizes in the country ; and the London hangman may get the jobs if he likes . He 's considered more skilful than any one else ; and , after all , practice makes perfect . As it is , he is forced to refuse a good many offers , because he ca n't be here , there , and everywhere . Now if Gibbet would only take to the business kindly , he might help his father to earn a fortune ! "
8848	8867	8869	8869	Markham	612	" But if the poor lad have a loathing for the horrible avocation — as well he may , "
8875	8885	8869	8869	Markham	612	" why should he be forced to embrace it ? "
8886	8896	8898	8899	the constable	1302	" Because he can never do himself good elsewhere , "
8901	8958	8898	8899	the constable	1302	" Who will employ the son of Jack Ketch ? Why , will you believe it , sir , that not a soul visits Smithers ' family ? Although he lives in this neighbourhood , where , God knows , people ai n't over nice and partickler , not a human being would cross his threshold . "
8959	8969	8971	8971	Markham	612	" Does that aversion arise from disgust or superstition ? "
8973	8979	8608	8608	Richard	612	" From both , sir , "
8984	9071	8608	8608	Richard	612	" The people that live in this district are of two kinds — the poor and ignorant , and the rogues and vagabonds . The poor and ignorant are afraid of the public executioner ; and the rogues and vagabonds hate him , although he 's merely an instrument . Miss Kate goes to market for him ; and the shop - keepers that know who she is , are scarcely civil to her . They seem as if they 'd rather she 'd keep away . "
9072	9084	9086	9086	Markham	612	" And you say that she is the executioner 's niece ? "
9088	9094	8608	8608	Richard	612	" Smithers says so himself , "
9099	9134	8608	8608	Richard	612	" and of course I know nothing to the contrary ; but it does seem strange that so amiable , genteel , and clever , a young gal should belong to such a family ! "
9135	9145	8608	8608	Richard	612	" Her own parents are dead , I presume ? "
9146	9307	8608	8608	Richard	612	" Yes , sir,—she is an orphan . When Smithers is very dull and miserable with his lonely situation , he sometimes comes down to the station and has a chat with us constables ; and then he 's pretty communicative . He told me one day that Katherine 's parents had died when she was very young , and so he was compelled to take care of her . All the while she was a child Smithers let her do pretty well as she liked ; and it is a wonder that she has turned out a good gal . But she regularly frequented the School established in the parish of Saint David 's by the Rev. Mr. Tracy ; and in that way she picked up a tolerable smattering of knowledge . Since then she 's instructed herself as much as she could , and has bought books with the little money that her needle has produced her . "
9308	9338	9340	9340	Richard	612	" But who employs her as a sempstress , if , as you say , so terrible a stigma affixes itself to each member of the hangman 's family ? "
9342	9364	9366	9367	the policeman	1274	" The old housekeeper at Mr. Tracy 's is very friendly disposed towards the poor creature , and gives her work , "
9369	9406	9366	9367	the policeman	1274	" Katherine does all she can to console that poor hump - back Gibbet ; and she has taught him to read and write — aye , and what 's more , sir , to pray . "
9407	9410	9412	9412	Richard	612	" Policeman , "
9418	9491	9412	9412	Richard	612	" the manner in which you have spoken relative to that poor girl , shows me that you have a good heart . Is there any mode of ameliorating her wretched situation ? I feel the deepest compassion for her miserable lot ; and all you have told me of her excellent character makes me anxious to see her removed from the vile society of that ruffian under whose roof she lives . "
9492	9512	9514	9515	the constable	1302	" I believe she is anxious to go out to service , sir , or open a little school , "
9517	9541	9514	9515	the constable	1302	" but her family connection is against her . Or else I do n't think that Smithers would care about parting with her . "
9542	9554	9556	9556	Markham	612	" What induces you to suppose that such are her wishes ? "
9558	9567	9366	9367	the policeman	1274	" Because she told me so , sir , "
9572	9602	9366	9367	the policeman	1274	" One evening I went to Smithers ' house , with a certain message from the Sheriff of London — you can guess what , I dare say — — "
9603	9620	9412	9412	Richard	612	" To acquaint him with the day fixed for some wretch 's execution , no doubt ? "
9621	9802	9366	9367	the policeman	1274	" Precisely , sir ; but Smithers was n't at home , and so I sate down and waited for him . It was n't in Jack Ketch 's own room up stairs where we went just now , and where he teaches his son how to hang by means of that puppet ; but it was in a little parlour they have got down stairs , and which Miss Kate keeps as clean and comfortable as if they saw no end of company . Well , I got talking to the young gal ; and though she never said a single word against her uncle , but spoke of him in a grateful and kind manner , she let out that if he _ could _ spare her , she should like to earn her own bread by her own exertions . And then the poor creature burst out crying , and said , that no one would take her as a servant , and that she should get no scholars even if she was to open a school . "
9820	9824	9826	9827	the policeman	1274	" Poor gal ! "
9836	10101	9826	9827	the policeman	1274	" she do n't deserve to suffer as she does . My beat is about this quarter : and I know pretty well all that 's going on . I see more than other people about here , because I 've opportunity and leisure . Besides , it 's my business . Well , sir , I can assure you that there is n't a more charitable or generous - hearted gal in all London than Miss Katherine . If a poor neighbour 's ill , it 's ten to one but some female muffled up in her shawl knocks at the door of the sick person 's house , leaves a parcel , and runs away ; and then there 's tea , and sugar , and gruel , for the invalid — and no one knows who brought it , or where it comes from . Or if a family 's in want , the baker calls with bread that 's paid for , but wo n't say who sent it . Or may be it 's the butcher with a small joint — but always sent in the same quiet manner . Then , while the poor creatures whose hearts are made glad by this unlooked - for charity , are wondering whether it was the parson , or the parson 's wife , or this benevolent gentleman , or that good lady , who sent the things , Kate buries herself in her room , and does n't even think that she has done any thing out of the way . "
